<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Range — an absolute value function with a vertical shift</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the function \( g(x) = {@gdisp@} \).</p>
<p>State the range of \(g\) as an inequality in \(y\).</p>
<div>\(y \geq\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>The <strong>range</strong> of \(g\) is the set of its output values. An absolute value is a distance, so it is never negative: \({@abs(x-a)@} \ge 0\), with smallest value \(0\) when \(x={@a@}\). Adding the vertical shift of \({@b@}\) moves every output by the same amount, so the smallest value of \(g(x)\) is \(0 + ({@b@}) = {@b@}\), and every larger value is also reached. So the range is \(y \geq {@b@}\).</p>
<div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="V-shaped graph of y = g(x) with its vertex marked and a dashed horizontal line through the vertex.">
[[jsxgraph width="320px" height="220px"]]
(function() {
  var a = {#a#};
  var b = {#b#};
  var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [a - 6, b + 7, a + 6, b - 2],
    axis: true, grid: false,
    showNavigation: false, showCopyright: false
  });
  board.create('functiongraph', [function(x) { return Math.abs(x - a) + b; }], {strokeColor: '#003399', strokeWidth: 2});
  board.create('line', [[a - 6, b], [a + 6, b]], {strokeColor: '#cc0000', dash: 2, strokeWidth: 1, fixed: true});
  board.create('point', [a, b], {size: 4, color: '#cc0000', fixed: true, name: 'vertex', label: {offset: [10, -15], anchorX: 'left'}});
})();
[[/jsxgraph]]
</div>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
